oviding evidence that the learned representation is relatively robust to content variations. 3.2 Model Architecture Guided by the above relative transformation formulation, we propose a dual-branch architecture as illustrated in Figure 2 . 3.2.1 Spectrogram Frontend For protocol compatibility, we retain the Fx-Encoder++ frontend [ 30 ] : both inputs are converted to log-mel spectrograms using an STFT with n fft = 2048 n_{\\text{fft}}=2048 and hop size 512. Following CNN14 [ 6 ] , we use 64 mel bins (50–18,000 Hz), log-scaling, and normalization to [ − 1 , 1 ] [-1,1] . 3.2.2 Siamese CNN Backbone We employ a CNN14-style [ 6 ] backbone with six convolution stages, channel dimensions [ 64 , 128 , 256 , 512 , 1024 , 2048 ] [64,128,256,512,1024,2048] . The two branches share all weights (Siamese configuration [ 20 ] ), ensuring that both signals are encoded in the same representation space. 3.2.3 Cross-Attention Interaction To enable each branch to incorporate the intermediate features of the other and better model the transformation between the two signals, we insert bidirectional cross-attention after stages 3 (256 channels) and 5 (1024 channels) . Each block uses 4 heads, 4 × 4\\times spatial pooling to limit memory, and residual scale 0.1 to preserve backbone features: 𝒉 ref ′ = 𝒉 ref + α ⋅ CrossAttn ​ ( 𝒉 ref , 𝒉 proc ) \\bm{h}_{\\text{ref}}^{\\prime}=\\bm{h}_{\\text{ref}}+\\alpha\\cdot\\text{CrossAttn}(\\bm{h}_{\\text{ref}},\\bm{h}_{\\text{proc}}) (3) where α = 0.1 \\alpha=0.1 . The cross-attention is applied symmetrically to both branches [ 10 , 15 ] . 3.2.4 Differential Gating Fusion After global pooling, we obtain embeddings e ref , e proc ∈ ℝ 2048 e_{\\mathrm{ref}},\\,e_{\\mathrm{proc}}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{2048} . We propose two fusion variants that combine the two branches into a single effect embedding. Base model. Our base fusion mechanism uses a learned gate to balance the relative change signal with the absolute processed signal: 𝒆 fx = 𝒈 ⊙ ( 𝒆 proc − 𝒆 ref ) + ( 1 − 𝒈 ) ⊙ 𝒆 proc \\bm{e}_{\\text{fx}}=\\bm{g}\\odot(\\bm{e}_{\\text{proc}}-\\bm{e}_{\\text{ref}})+(1-\\bm{g})\\odot\\bm{e}_{\\text{proc}} (4) 𝒈 = σ ​ ( 𝑾 ​ [ 𝒆 ref ; 𝒆 proc ] + 𝒃 ) \\bm{g}=\\sigma(\\bm{W}[\\bm{e}_{\\text{ref}};\\bm{e}_{\\text{proc}}]+\\bm{b}) (5) where σ \\sigma is the sigmoid function and [ ⋅ ; ⋅ ] [\\cdot;\\cdot] denotes concatenation. The differential term captures the effect-induced relative change between signals, while the absolute term retains the content and timbral characteristics of the processed signal [ 31 ] . The gate adaptively balances these two sources depending on the effect type. Bidirectional-compatible variant. To enforce antisymmetry at the fusion stage, we revise the fusion mechanism with two key adjustments: (1) the concatenat", "stderr": "\nShell cwd was reset to /inspire/qb-ilm/project/video-generation/chenxie-25019/hyr/iHateTheWorld555.github.io", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
