{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-90098c216180a7c8", "type": "tool_result", "content": "Bidirectional-compatible variant. To enforce antisymmetry at the fusion stage, we revise the fusion mechanism with two key adjustments: (1) the concatenation-based gate input is replaced with element-wise addition, making the gate output invariant to input order; (2) we remove the standalone static branch term 𝒆 proc \\bm{e}_{\\text{proc}} and retain only the differential interaction term: 𝒆 fx = 𝒈 ⊙ ( 𝒆 proc − 𝒆 ref ) \\bm{e}_{\\text{fx}}=\\bm{g}\\odot(\\bm{e}_{\\text{proc}}-\\bm{e}_{\\text{ref}}) (6) 𝒈 = σ ​ ( 𝑾 ​ ( 𝒆 ref + 𝒆 proc ) + 𝒃 ) \\bm{g}=\\sigma(\\bm{W}(\\bm{e}_{\\text{ref}}+\\bm{e}_{\\text{proc}})+\\bm{b}) (7) Since the gate 𝒈 \\bm{g} is order-invariant and the differential term ( 𝒆 proc − 𝒆 ref ) (\\bm{e}_{\\text{proc}}-\\bm{e}_{\\text{ref}}) reverses its sign when swapping the two branch embeddings, the fusion output is antisymmetric by construction. Implications for bidirectional encoding. A practical consequence of the antisymmetric fusion is that swapping an ordered input pair yields an approximately sign-reversed relation embedding: E ​ ( A , B ) ≈ − E ​ ( B , A ) . E(A,B)\\approx-E(B,A). (8) For the waveform-aligned case B = Fx ⁡ ( A ; θ ) B=\\operatorname{Fx}(A;\\theta) , this represents the reverse direction of the same input–output relation. The antisymmetric variant therefore provides a unified, direction-aware representation of relative effect changes. In our experiments, we use the base model for the main style-transfer evaluation and separately train the antisymmetric variant to evaluate this structural property. 3.2.5 Projection Head A two-layer MLP projects 𝒆 fx \\bm{e}_{\\text{fx}} to a 128-dimensional embedding with L2 normalization: 𝒛 = L2Norm ​ ( MLP ​ ( 𝒆 fx ) ) ∈ ℝ 128 \\bm{z}=\\text{L2Norm}(\\text{MLP}(\\bm{e}_{\\text{fx}}))\\in\\mathbb{R}^{128} . The base model uses ReLU activation. For the bidirectional-compatible variant, we replace ReLU with tanh \\tanh —an odd function satisfying tanh ⁡ ( − x ) = − tanh ⁡ ( x ) \\tanh(-x)=-\\tanh(x) —which helps preserve the sign-reversal property through projection. Because the linear layers include learned biases, antisymmetry of the final L2-normalized embedding is approximate and is evaluated empirically in Sec. 4.4 . Figure 1 : Illustration of our contrastive learning framework. Positive pairs share the same effects transformation θ \\theta applied to different audio content, while negative pairs use independently sampled configurations. Cross-segment sampling ensures content-effect disentanglement. 3.3 Training 3.3.1 Data Preparation Figure 1 summarizes the training-pair construction. For each training item, we sample two distinct audio tracks indexed by q ∈ { a , b } q\\in\\{a,b\\} , a pair of non-overlapping clips ( x q , x q ′ ) (x_{q},x^{\\prime}_{q}) from each track, and one effect configuration θ \\theta . We encode 𝒛 q ​ ( θ ) = E ​ ( x q , Fx ​ ( x q ′ , θ ) ) \\bm{z}_{q}(\\theta)=E(x_{q},\\text{Fx}(x^{\\prime}_{q},\\theta)) ; ( 𝒛 a ​ ( θ ) , 𝒛 b ​ ( θ ) ) (\\bm{z}_{a}(\\theta),\\bm{z}_{b}(\\theta)) is positive, whereas negatives use a different configuration θ ′ \\theta^{\\prime} . For full-mix audio, the two tracks come from different songs; for MoisesDB, they may also be different stems from the same song. Cross-segment sampling. Within an observation, ( x q , x q ′ ) (x_{q},x^{\\prime}_{q}) are adjacent, non-overlapping clips from one structural section (e.g., verse), retaining related musical context without sample-wise waveform alignment. This within-observation pairing discourages content-based shortcuts, whereas positive observations are formed from independently sampled tracks. Figure 2 : RelFx architecture. Weight-shared CNN branches exchange features through bidirectional cross-attention before Diff-Gate fusion. The antisymmetric variant uses the fusion in (Eqs. 6 - 7 ) and the projection in Sec. 3.2.5. Segment-level content analysis. Table 1 provides a content-similarity analysis supporting this sampling choice. For Fx-Encoder++ embeddings [ 30 ] over 2,000 songs, mean similarity falls from ≥ 0.918 \\geq 0.918 for same-section pairs (A/B), to 0.783–0.838 across sections (C/D), and 0.684 across songs (E). These results support using nearby clips from the same song section, since they contain some waveform variation while still sharing similar musical content. RelFx is also evaluated on song-disjoint splits and MUSDB18. A systematic evaluation of its robustness across different instruments is left for future work. 3.3.2 Effects Chain Following Fx-Encoder++ [ 30 ] , we use a chain of 8 sequential effects (equalizer, distortion, multiband compressor, gain, stereo imager, limiter, delay, reverb) [ 26 ] , resulting in 72 continuous parameters and 8 binary activation switches. The effect order is randomly shuffled per batch; each effect is activated with a dynamically adjusted probability during training (detailed below). Dynamic Fx probability scheduling. Instead of fixed activation probabilities, we adaptively adjust each effect’s activation probability based on its running training loss. At the end of each epoch, we compute the mean parameter regression loss for each effect over the past epoch, then renormalize probabilities such that effects with higher residual loss are sampled more frequently in the next epoch. All probabilities are clamped to [ p min , p max ] = [ 0.3 , 0.8 ] [p_{\\text{min}},p_{\\text{max}}]=[0.3,0.8] to maintain diversity and avoid single-effect dominance. Initialized with uniform probabilities, this strategy is updated throughout training, and its contribution is analyzed in Sec. 4.3 . 3.3.3 Loss Function Our combined loss consists of three components: ℒ = ℒ ctr + λ t ​ ℒ triplet + λ p ​ ℒ param \\mathcal{L}=\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{ctr}}+\\lambda_{t}\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{triplet}}+\\lambda_{p}\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{param}} (9) Contrastive loss. For N N positive pairs, let p ​ ( i ) p(i) denote the index of the positive counterpart of z i z_{i} among the 2 ​ N 2N embeddings, and define s i ​ k = sim ⁡ ( z i , z k ) / τ s_{ik}=\\operatorname{sim}(z_{i},z_{k})/\\tau using cosine similarity and τ = 0.15 \\tau=0.15 . We use the following modified NT-Xent objective [ 1 ] , where the positive logit is included twice in the denominator: ℒ ctr = − 1 2 ​ N ​ ∑ i = 1 2 ​ N log ⁡ e s i , p ​ ( i ) 2 ​ e s i , p ​ ( i ) + ∑ k = 1 k ∉ { i , p ​ ( i ) } 2 ​ N e s i ​ k . \\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{ctr}}=-\\frac{1}{2N}\\sum_{i=1}^{2N}\\log\\frac{e^{s_{i,p(i)}}}{2e^{s_{i,p(i)}}+\\sum_{\\begin{subarray}{c}k=1\\\\ k\\notin\\{i,p(i)\\}\\end{subarray}}^{2N}e^{s_{ik}}}. (10) Triplet loss [ 11 ] provides explicit margin-based separation with margin m = 0.3 m=0.3 : ℒ triplet = 1 N ​ ∑ i = 1 N max ⁡ ( 0 , d ​ ( 𝒛 i a , 𝒛 i b ) − d ​ ( 𝒛 i a , 𝒛 i neg ) + m ) \\mathcal{L}_{\\text{triplet}}=\\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\max(0,d(\\bm{z}_{i}^{a},\\bm{z}_{i}^{b})-d(\\bm{z}_{i}^{a},\\bm{z}_{i}^{\\text{neg}})+m) (11) where d ​ ( ⋅ , ⋅ ) d(\\cdot,\\cdot) denotes cosine distance, z i a z_{i}^{a} and z i b z_{i}^{b} form the i i -th positive pair, and z i neg = E ​ ( x i , Fx ⁡ ( x i ′ ; θ i ′ ) ) z_{i}^{\\mathrm{neg}}=E\\bigl(x_{i},\\operatorname{Fx}(x^{\\prime}_{i};\\theta^{\\prime}_{i})\\bigr) reuses the anchor clips with θ i ′ ≠ θ i \\theta^{\\prime}_{i}\\neq\\theta_{i} . The triplet term enforces a margin against this content-matched negative. While the contrastive term already uses all in-batch negatives, the triplet term provides an explicit margin against this controlled hard negative, enforcing a minimum required embedding margin between content-matched observations with distinct effect parameters. Parameter regression loss enforces continuous structure in the embedding space. Let 𝒑 \\bm{p} denote the 72-dimensional vector of continuous effect parameters and 𝒂 \\bm{a} the eight activation switches. An auxiliary head predicts both quantities from the embedding: ℒ param = MSE masked ⁡ ( 𝒑 ^ , 𝒑 ) + λ a ​ BCE ⁡ ( 𝒂 ^ , 𝒂 ) . \\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{param}}=\\operatorname{MSE}_{\\mathrm{masked}}\\bigl(\\hat{\\bm{p}},\\bm{p}\\bigr)+\\lambda_{a}\\operatorname{BCE}\\bigl(\\hat{\\bm{a}},\\bm{a}\\bigr). (12) The masked MSE is computed only over parameters associatedwith activated effects. We set ( λ t = 0.5 \\lambda_{t}=0.5 ), ( λ p = 0.5 \\lambda_{p}=0.5 ), and ( λ a = 0.3 \\lambda_{a}=0.3 ), and use ( τ = 0.15 \\tau=0.15 ) and ( m = 0.3 m=0.3 ). All hyper parameters are fixed across ablations. 4 Experiments 4.1 Experimental Setup (a) single-input (b) RelFx (dual-input) Figure 3 : ITO pipelines for Fx style transfer. Both paradigms optimize the effect-chain parameters θ {\\theta} of a differentiable DSP using gradients ∇ θ \\nabla{\\theta} derived from an embedding loss. 4.1.1 Training Data We train on audio from two sources: (1) a licensed internal collection of 6,447 mixed tracks (2,681 songs, 185.9 h), and (2) 2,585 stems from MoisesDB [ 18 ] (240 songs, 156.4 h). During training, 10-second stereo clips are sampled at 44.1 kHz, matching the sampling rate used by the DSP chain and the evaluation protocol; full-mix clips come from verse and chorus sections identified by music structure analysis. The training and validation sets are split by song ID (85% / 15%) with a fixed split seed of 42. For MoisesDB, we apply content density filtering (requiring ≥ \\geq 70% non-silent frames) to exclude sparse regions during clip sampling. 4.1.2 Training Details We train for 200 epochs on 4 × 4\\times NVIDIA L20 GPUs, batch size 48 per GPU and gradient accumulation of 4 steps (effective batch size 768). We use AdamW [ 12 ] with learning rate 5 × 10 − 4 5\\times 10^{-4} , weight decay 10 − 5 10^{-5} , 10-epoch linear warmup, and cosine annealing to 10 − 6 10^{-6} . 4.1.3 Baselines We compare against two categories of methods: effect-specific representation models including Fx-Encoder++ [ 30 ] and AFx-Rep [ 25 ] , and general-purpose audio representation models including VGGish [ 4 ] and CLAP [ 29 ] . All baselines are evaluated using their official models and standard protocols. 4.1.4 Evaluation Task Fx style transfer. Our primary task is embedding-guided Fx style transfer, following the settings of Fx-Encoder++ [ 30 ] . Given a reference audio with target effects, we use the encoder’s embedding to guide gradient-based optimization of a differentiable effect chain, recovering the reference’s processing from a clean source signal. To avoid local optima, each sample is optimized with 7 random restarts using Adam (learning rate 10 − 2 10^{-2} , cosine annealing) for up to 200 iterations with early stopping (patience 50), and the restart with the lowest L d L_{d} is retained. We report the median multi-resolution STFT loss L d L_{d} (lower is better) between the optimized output and the ground-truth target on MUSDB18 [ 19 ] , using the same 100 samples per instrument for every method. These samples are processed with Fx-Normalization [ 14 ] solely to align with the Fx-Encoder++ evaluation protocol; RelFx representation training itself does not require normalization. Effects retrieval (for ablation). We also adopt the effects retrieval protocol from Fx-Encoder++ [ 30 ] as a supplementary evaluation. Given a processed query clip, we retrieve candidates with identical effects but distinct content from 500 samples, ranked by cosine similarity. Performance is reported as Recall@ K K ( K K =1,5,10), where a match in top- K K counts as correct. We evaluate on MUSDB18 [ 19 ] over four instruments with 1–8 effect chains, averaging results across settings. This metric acts as a coarse-grained ablation indicator, while L d L_{d} remains our primary metric. 4.2 Fx Style Transfer Results Table 2 presents the Fx style transfer results, and Figure 3 illustrates the ITO pipelines for both paradigms. We first detail the three input configurations for our model (used in the evaluation) before