during training. Removing it yields the second-largest L d L_{d} degradation (1.399 → \\to 1.465) while again improving retrieval R@1 (67.3 → \\to 71.7), as upweighting under-learned effects ensures balanced parameter estimation across all effect types. Single-branch removes the reference branch and encodes only the processed signal, matching the absolute encoding paradigm of Fx-Encoder++ [ 30 ] . This leads to retrieval degradation ( − - 21.2 R@1, 67.3 → \\to 46.1) and increases L d L_{d} to 1.457 ( + 4.1 % +4.1\\% ), demonstrating the clear advantage of our dual-branch relative formulation. Notably, removing cross-segment sampling, parameter regression, or dynamic scheduling increases R@1 while degrading style transfer. This indicates that retrieval accuracy alone may not fully reflect style-transfer performance and is therefore insufficient as the sole metric for effect representations. 4.4 Bidirectional Encoding Properties Beyond the base model, we additionally train the antisymmetric variant (Eq. 6 ) under identical configurations. As shown in Table 2 ( Bi-dir. ), it achieves style-transfer performance on par with the base model (Avg L d = 1.389 L_{d}=1.389 ), without sacrificing accuracy under its structural constraint. A key empirical property of this variant is that swapping the input order produces an approximately sign-reversed embedding of the reverse-direction relation (Eq. 8 ). We evaluate this behavior on 100 triplets per subset from the MUSDB18 drums and vocals subsets using two structural properties: 1. Antisymmetry : cos ⁡ ( E ​ ( A , B ) , E ​ ( B , A ) ) ≈ − 1 \\cos(E(A,B),E(B,A))\\!\\approx\\!{-}1 2. Composition : cos ⁡ ( E A ​ B + E B ​ C , E A ​ C ) ≈ 1 \\cos(E_{AB}\\!+\\!E_{BC},\\,E_{AC})\\!\\approx\\!1 Table 4 : Bidirectional encoding properties evaluated on the MUSDB18 drums and vocals subsets. RelFx (Bi-dir.) denotes the separately trained antisymmetric variant (Eq. 6 ), while RelFx (Base) denotes the base model (Eq. 4 ). Property Drums Voc